Unit 4: UI Components
=====================
4.1 System menus and functions of menus
=======================================
What is a System Menu?
======================
A system menu is a menu provided by an operating system or application that contains
essential commands to control a window or program.

It usually appears when you:
Click the icon in the top-left corner of a window
Press a shortcut (like Alt + Space in Windows)

Example system: Windows 11

Common System Menu Options
==========================
A typical system menu includes:

1. Restore
==========
Returns a maximized window to its previous size.

2. Move
=======
Allows repositioning the window on the screen.

3. Size
=======
Lets the user resize the window manually.

4. Minimize
===========
Sends the window to the taskbar.

5. Maximize
===========
Expands the window to full screen.

6. Close
========
Closes the application.

Functions of Menus in User Interfaces
=====================================
Menus are used to organize commands and improve usability.

Main Functions:
1. Navigation
=============
Helps users move between screens or pages.

Example:
Home → Profile → Settings

2. Command Execution
====================
Menus allow users to perform actions.

Example:
========
Save file
Print document
Delete item

3. Organization of Features
===========================
Groups related functions together.

Example:
=======
File menu: Open, Save, Exit
Edit menu: Cut, Copy, Paste

4. System Control
=================
Controls window or application behavior.

Example:
========
Minimize
Maximize
Close

5. Quick Access
===============
Provides shortcuts to frequently used actions.

Example:
========
Toolbar menus in software like Microsoft Word


Types of Menus in Interfaces
============================
1. System Menu
Controls the window itself (move, close, resize)

2. Application Menu
Contains program functions (file, edit, view)

3. Context Menu
Appears on right-click (copy, paste, delete)

4. Navigation Menu
Used in websites and apps (home, about, contact)

5. Flyout or Cascading Menu
6. Breadcrumb Menu
7. Mega Menu
8. Radial Menu


4.2 Formatting of menus: Consistency, display, presentation, organization, complexity
    , item arrangement, ordering, grouping
=====================================================================================
Menu formatting refers to the visual organization and styling of menus in a user 
interface so they are easy to read, navigate, and use.

Good menu formatting improves:
==============================
Usability
Readability
Navigation speed
User experience

Formatting of Menus: Consistency
================================
Consistency in menu formatting means that menus should look and behave the same 
throughout the interface. Users should not have to relearn navigation on every 
screen.

What Consistency Means in Menus
===============================
Consistent menus use:
Same layout
Same colors
Same fonts
Same spacing
Same interaction behavior

Example:
========
If the “Home” menu is blue on one page, it should not suddenly become green on another page.

Formatting of Menus: Display
===========================
Display in menu formatting refers to how menu items are visually presented on the 
screen so users can easily see, understand, and interact with them.

Good menu display improves:
==========================
Visibility
Readability
Navigation speed
User experience

Formatting of Menus: Presentation
=================================
Presentation in menu formatting refers to the visual appearance and arrangement of 
menu items so they are attractive, readable, and easy to use.

Good presentation improves:
===========================
User experience
Navigation clarity
Readability
Professional appearance

Formatting of Menus: Organization
=================================
Organization in menu formatting means arranging menu items in a logical, structured,
and meaningful way so users can quickly find what they need.

Good menu organization improves:
================================
Navigation
Usability
Efficiency
User understanding

Menus should group related options together and arrange them in a clear order.

Example:
========
File
 ├── New
 ├── Open
 ├── Save
 └── Exit

Related commands are grouped under File.

Formatting of Menus: Complexity
===============================
Complexity in menu formatting refers to the level of difficulty users experience 
while understanding and navigating menus.

A menu becomes complex when it contains:
Too many options
Deep submenu levels
Confusing organization
Unclear labels

Good UI design aims to reduce unnecessary complexity.

Formatting of Menus: Item arrangement
=====================================
Item arrangement in menu formatting refers to how menu items are ordered, grouped, 
and positioned within a menu so users can quickly locate and use commands.

Good arrangement improves:
==========================
Navigation speed
Readability
Usability
User experience
Meaning of Item Arrangement

Menu items should be placed:
Logically
Consistently
In meaningful order

Formatting of Menus: Ordering
=============================
Ordering in menu formatting refers to the sequence in which menu items are arranged
so users can find commands quickly and naturally.

Good ordering improves:
=======================
Navigation speed
Usability
User understanding
Efficiency

Formatting of Menus: Grouping
=============================
Grouping in menu formatting means arranging related menu items together under a 
common category or section so users can easily understand and find functions.
It reduces confusion and improves navigation by organizing similar commands.

Meaning of Menu Grouping
=========================
Instead of listing all items randomly, related items are placed together.

Example:
========
File
 ├── New
 ├── Open
 ├── Save
 └── Exit
Here, all file-related actions are grouped under File.

4.3 Types of menus: Menu bar, pull down menu, cascading menu, popup menu, tear off
    menu, iconic menu
==================================================================================
A Menu Bar is a horizontal strip (usually at the top of a window or application) 
that contains a list of main menu options. Each option can be clicked to reveal 
a dropdown menu with more commands.

It is one of the most common navigation structures in desktop applications.

Where Menu Bars Are Used
========================

Menu bars are commonly found in:
Desktop software
Web applications (less common now)
IDEs and productivity tools

Example systems:
================
Microsoft Word
Adobe Photoshop
Windows 11 apps

Structure of a Menu Bar
Example:
=======
File   Edit   View   Insert   Format   Tools   Help

Each item opens a dropdown list of commands.


Types of menus: Pull down menu
==============================
A Pull-down menu is a type of menu that appears when a user clicks or hovers on a 
menu title (usually in a menu bar). It “pulls down” a list of related commands 
from the top. It is widely used in desktop applications and classic GUI systems.

How It Works
============
User clicks a menu item in the menu bar (e.g., File)
A vertical list drops down under it
User selects an option (e.g., Open, Save)

Example:
========
File ▼
   New
   Open
   Save
   Exit

Pull-down menus are common in:
==============================
Desktop applications
Office software
Graphics tools
System applications

Examples:
========
Microsoft Word
Adobe Photoshop
Windows 11 classic applications

Types of menus: Cascading menu
==============================
A Cascading Menu (also called a hierarchical menu) is a type of menu where 
selecting one option opens another submenu, which may open another submenu, 
forming a multi-level chain. It “cascades” sideways step by step.

How It Works
============
User opens a main menu
Hover/click on an item
A submenu appears to the side
Further submenus may open from that submenu

Example:
========
File
 └── Open
      └── Recent Files
           └── Project1.docx

Cascading menus are commonly found in:
======================================
Desktop applications
File systems
Settings panels
Complex software tools

Examples:
=========
Windows 11 Settings menus
Adobe Photoshop tool options
Microsoft Word advanced menus

Types of menus: Popup Menu
==========================
A Popup Menu (also called a context menu or right-click menu) is a menu that 
appears temporarily when the user performs a specific action, usually right-
clicking or long-pressing an object. It provides quick actions related to the 
selected item.

How It Works
============
User right-clicks on an object (file, text, image)
A small menu appears near the cursor
User selects an action
Menu disappears after selection or clicking outside

Example:
========
Right Click Menu:
------------------
Open
Copy
Paste
Delete
Properties

Popup menus are commonly used in:
================================
Desktop operating systems
File managers
Text editors
Web applications

Examples:
=========
Windows 11 file explorer
Microsoft Word editing options
Adobe Photoshop tool actions


Types of menus: Tear off menu
=============================
A Tear-off menu is a special type of menu that can be “detached” from its original 
position and turned into a floating window so it stays visible on the screen.

It helps users access frequently used commands without repeatedly opening the menu.

How It Works
============
User opens a menu (e.g., File, Edit, Tools)
A tear-off handle or dotted line appears at the top of the menu
User drags or clicks it
The menu “tears off” and becomes a separate floating panel

Example:
========
Tools Menu
------------
✂ Cut
📄 Copy
📋 Paste
🔧 Options   ← tear-off handle

Tear-off menus are mostly found in:
===================================
Older desktop GUI systems
Advanced graphics tools
Custom tool palettes

Examples:
=========
Adobe Photoshop tool palettes
GIMP floating tool windows
Legacy UNIX/X11 applications


Types of menus: Iconic menu
===========================
An Iconic Menu is a type of menu where options are represented using icons
(symbols or images) instead of text labels. Users select commands by clicking the 
icons.

It is widely used in modern graphical user interfaces because it is compact and 
visually intuitive.

How It Works
============
Each menu item is shown as an icon
Users click/tap the icon to perform an action
Often includes tooltips (text appears on hover)

Example:
========
💾    📂  ✏️   🗑️     🔍
Save Open Edit Delete Search


Iconic menus are commonly used in:
=================================
Mobile apps
Toolbars in desktop applications
Web applications
Design tools

Examples:
========
Adobe Photoshop toolbars
Microsoft Word quick access toolbar
Google Chrome navigation icons

4.4 Selection of windows and its components, window presentation styles: Tiled win-
    dows, overlapping windows, cascading windows
===================================================================================
Window selection means choosing a specific window to work on when multiple windows 
are open.

How windows are selected:
========================
Clicking anywhere inside a window
Using taskbar (switching apps)
Using keyboard shortcuts (Alt + Tab)
Touch selection on mobile systems

Active vs Inactive Window
=========================
Type	                 Meaning
====                     =======
Active Window	        Currently selected and in use
Inactive Window	        Open but not currently focused

Example:
========
You are typing in Microsoft Word → Word is active
Google Chrome is open in background → inactive

A window is made up of several standard GUI components.

1. Title Bar
Located at the top
Shows application name
Contains minimize, maximize, close buttons

Example:
Microsoft Word  |  _  □  ✖

2. Menu Bar
Contains menus like File, Edit, View
Provides access to commands

3. Tool Bar
Contains shortcut icons for common actions
Example: Save, Print, Undo

4. Work Area (Client Area)
Main area where content is displayed or edited
Example: document text area in Word

5. Scroll Bars
Vertical and horizontal bars
Used to navigate content

6. Status Bar
Located at bottom
Shows status information (page number, zoom level)

7. Borders and Corners
Used to resize the window
Drag edges to adjust size
Illustration of Window Components

3. Types of Windows in Selection Context
1. Primary Window

Main working window (e.g., document editor)

2. Secondary Window

Supporting window (e.g., settings)

3. Dialog Box

Small popup for input or message




